❯ does this require a certificate? 

● No. Here's why:

  tun2http does transparent tunneling, not interception. For HTTPS/WSS traffic it uses HTTP CONNECT to create a tunnel:

  App → TUN → tun2http → CONNECT httpbin.org:443 HTTP/1.1 → PdaNet → Internet
                           ↑ tunnel established, encrypted data passes through unchanged

  The C library never touches the TLS layer. It just rewrites TCP connections as proxy CONNECT requests. The certificate handshake happens end-to-end between the app and the remote server — tun2http is invisible to it.

  The only case a certificate would be needed is if you wanted to intercept and inspect HTTPS traffic (like Burp Suite does). That's not what we're doing — we just want to route traffic through the PdaNet proxy so apps can reach the internet.

  APK signing: Android needs a signed APK to install, but debug builds use the automatic debug keystore from Android Studio — no setup needed. ADB install works with debug builds.

❯ does the app ask for permissions when installed what permissions 

● At install: no dialogs. All the manifest permissions (INTERNET, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, ACCESS_WIFI_STATE, etc.) are normal permissions — Android grants them automatically at install with no prompt.

  The one permission that does prompt the user is the VPN permission — but it's not a manifest permission. It's a special system dialog triggered at runtime by VpnService.prepare(). That fires when the user taps Start in MainActivity:

  val intent = VpnService.prepare(this)
  if (intent != null) {
      startActivityForResult(intent, VPN_REQUEST_CODE)  // shows dialog
  }

  It shows once, looks like:

  "PdaNet Client wants to set up a VPN connection that allows it to monitor network traffic. Only allow this if you trust the source."

  After the user taps Allow, the permission is remembered. It won't ask again unless the app is reinstalled or another VPN app revokes it.

  Boot behavior: BootReceiver fires start() at boot, the service starts and registers the network callback — but establish() will return null until VPN permission has been granted at least once from the UI. So the user needs to open the app and tap Start once before boot-auto-connect will work.

❯ does it set up and tear down the TUN by itself? is it all automatic in    
  other words?                                                              

● Yes, fully automatic — after the one-time VPN permission tap.

  Setup:
  Boot → BootReceiver → service starts → registerNetworkCallback()
                                                ↓
                                actively watching all WiFi networks
                                                ↓
                           gateway == 192.168.49.1 detected?
                                                ↓ yes
                                          startTunnel()
                                          jni_start(fd, "192.168.49.1", 8000)
                                          → TUN up, all traffic routed

  Teardown:
  PdaNet disconnects → onLost() fires → stopTunnel() → jni_stop(fd) → fd.close()
                                              ↓
                                back to "Waiting for PdaNet…"
                                still watching for reconnect

  Also handles the case where you switch to a regular WiFi — onLinkPropertiesChanged fires, gateway is no longer 192.168.49.1, tunnel tears down gracefully.

  Service persistence: START_STICKY means if Android kills the service under memory pressure, it restarts automatically. The null intent case in onStartCommand re-registers the network callback.

  The only two manual steps ever needed:
  1. Tap Start once to grant VPN permission
  2. Never again — boot, connect, disconnect all handled automatically

❯ does it have a main activity? 

● Yes — MainActivity.kt with two buttons (Start/Stop) and a status text. It's minimal, just enough to trigger the one-time VPN permission dialog.

  It's not strictly needed for operation — the service runs headlessly. But without it there's no way to trigger VpnService.prepare() for that first permission grant.

  Options:

  1. Keep it as-is — simple UI, used once to grant permission, service does everything else
  2. Remove the UI entirely — but then you'd need another way to trigger the VPN permission dialog (could do it from the BootReceiver by launching a transparent activity just for that)
  3. Auto-trigger permission on boot — BootReceiver launches a transparent one-shot activity that calls VpnService.prepare(), grants permission, then immediately finishes — no visible UI at all after that

